What makes it agentic
Capability	How it is implemented
Autonomy in routing	The Planner LLM chooses the next worker on every loop. There is no fixed pipeline.
Tool selection by the LLM	Workers are ReAct-style agents. The LLM sees the MCP tool schemas and decides which tool to call and with what arguments.
Self-correction (inside an agent)	GeocodeWorker retries geocode_location with other spellings or a country (e.g. Dilli → Delhi).
Replanning (across agents)	A failed worker becomes an observation. The Planner then retries with a different instruction, changes approach, or explains the failure honestly.
Memory	LangGraph MemorySaver per thread_id. Final answers are stored as AIMessages so follow-up questions have context.
Standard tool layer	Every external call is an MCP tool on the local server over stdio.
Architecture
┌──────────────┐  HTTP   ┌──────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────────┐
│ Streamlit UI │ ──────► │ FastAPI backend  │ ─────► │ LangGraph workflow       │
│   (app.py)   │ ◄────── │   (backend.py)   │ ◄───── │ (workflow.py + agents.py)│
└──────────────┘  JSON   └──────────────────┘        └────────────┬─────────────┘
                                                                  │
                          ┌───────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────┐
                          ▼                                       ▼                ▼
                 ┌───────────────────┐                  ┌───────────────┐   ┌────────────┐
                 │ Worker agents     │  stdio (MCP)     │ Planner LLM   │   │ Worker LLM │
                 │ Geocode/Weather/  │ ───────────────► │ (supervisor)  │   │ (Groq,     │
                 │ Safety            │                  └───────────────┘   │ tool-call) │
                 └─────────┬─────────┘                                      └────────────┘
                           ▼
                 ┌───────────────────────────┐
                 │ MCP server (mcp_server.py)│
                 └──┬─────────┬──────────┬───┘
                    ▼         ▼          ▼
             Nominatim   Open-Meteo   Local risk
             (OSM)       (weather)    rules

The MCP server and client run on the same machine. A worker launches mcp_server.py as a child process over stdio, so there is no extra port or process to manage.

Workflow

The graph is hub-and-spoke. The Planner is called again after every worker.

GeocodeWorker
WeatherFetchWorker
SafetyWorker
FinalResponse
result or failure
result or failure
result or failure
START
🧭 Planner
📍 GeocodeWorker
🌡️ WeatherFetchWorker
🛡️ SafetyWorker
END
The loop
A question arrives. The backend calls the graph with the session's thread_id.
Planner observes the question, the collected data, the observation log and the attempt counts.
Planner decides one step and returns structured output: reasoning → next_worker → task_instruction → final_answer (only when finishing).
A worker agent runs. It opens an MCP session, lets the LLM pick tools, then reports success or failure through a finish tool.
The result is written to state as an observation, plus data on success.
Back to step 2. The Planner replans using the new observation. The loop ends when the Planner chooses FinalResponse or a guardrail stops it.
Routing examples (decided by the LLM, not hard-coded)
User question	Typical path
"Where is Pune?"	Planner → 📍 → Planner
"Weather at 28.61, 77.21"	Planner → 🌡️ → Planner
"Weather in Dilli tomorrow"	Planner → 📍 (self-corrects Dilli→Delhi) → Planner → 🌡️ (get_daily_forecast) → Planner
"Is it safe to fly a drone in Mumbai?"	Planner → 📍 → Planner → 🌡️ → Planner → 🛡️ → Planner
"Hi!"	Planner
"What's the weather?" (no city)	Planner asks for a location
Agents
🧭 Planner (planner_agent): the supervisor
Called on every loop. It reads the state, observations and attempts, then picks the next worker or FinalResponse.
Writes a task instruction for the worker. After a failure it must change the instruction (alternate spelling, country, different approach).
Writes the final answer grounded only in collected data. If it returns an empty answer, a fallback composer writes one.
Resets per-question state when a new user message arrives, so one city's data never leaks into another answer.
Enforces guardrails (see Guardrails).
📍 GeocodeWorker: autonomous geocoding agent
MCP tool: geocode_location (Nominatim).
Chooses the query, inspects candidates, and retries up to 3 times with other spellings, the English name, or a country.
Reports through finish_geocoding(status, summary, latitude, longitude, place_name). Coordinates are validated (lat −90…90, lon −180…180) before use.
If the model forgets to finish, the runtime salvages the top Nominatim match.
🌡️ WeatherFetchWorker: autonomous weather agent
MCP tools: get_forecast (current) and get_daily_forecast (1–7 days).
The LLM picks the tool that fits the question (now vs tomorrow vs weekend), or both.
Uses known coordinates or coordinates typed by the user, and never invents them. With none, it reports failure so the Planner can route to GeocodeWorker.
Stores the raw tool outputs in raw_weather_payload.
🛡️ SafetyWorker: hybrid rules + LLM agent
MCP tool: assess_weather_risk, a deterministic rule engine (wind, precipitation, temperature, humidity thresholds).
The LLM reads the numbers from the weather JSON, calls the tool, then adds context-specific alerts and a short summary through finish_safety.
If no weather data exists yet, it fails immediately and the Planner replans to fetch weather first.
MCP Tools and Data Sources

All tools live in mcp_server.py (stdio transport).

MCP tool	Called by	Data source	Purpose
geocode_location(query, limit)	GeocodeWorker	Nominatim (OpenStreetMap): https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search	Place name → coordinates (JSON candidates)
get_forecast(latitude, longitude)	WeatherFetchWorker	Open-Meteo: https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast	Current temperature, humidity, wind speed, precipitation
get_daily_forecast(latitude, longitude, days)	WeatherFetchWorker	Open-Meteo (daily variables)	Max/min temperature, precipitation sum, max wind for 1–7 days
assess_weather_risk(temp, wind, precip, humidity)	SafetyWorker	Local rule engine (no network)	risk_level, drone_flight verdict, alerts

Other components:

Component	Role
Groq openai/gpt-oss-20b (via get_groq_llm)	Worker agents: tool calling and the fallback final-answer composer
Planner LLM (via get_structured_planner_llm)	Structured supervisor decisions (PlannerOutput)

Notes:

Nominatim and Open-Meteo are keyless. Nominatim policy: a valid User-Agent and at most about 1 request per second. The server enforces the rate limit and reads your contact from the NOMINATIM_USER_AGENT env var. Set it to a real address.
NOMINATIM_URL and OPEN_METEO_URL env vars can override the endpoints (handy for tests or mocks).
The risk thresholds are heuristics (for example drones: wind ≥ 40 km/h is NO-GO), not official aviation guidance.
Sample tool output
json
{"source":"Open-Meteo","latitude":28.61,"longitude":77.21,"time":"2026-10-25T12:00",
 "temperature_c":31.2,"humidity_pct":48,"wind_speed_kmh":12.5,"precipitation_mm":0.0}
Replanning and Self-Correction

Level 1: inside a worker (tool-level). The LLM sees a tool result and adapts.

geocode_location("Dilli")  -> results: []  (hint: try another spelling)
geocode_location("Delhi")  -> results: [Delhi, India ...]
finish_geocoding(success, 28.61, 77.21)

Level 2: across workers (plan-level). A failure is recorded as an observation and the Planner replans.

[GeocodeWorker] FAILED: no results for 'Xyzzyville'
Planner: retry with a different instruction ("try the nearest big city / ask for country")
        …or FinalResponse: "I couldn't find that place, could you check the spelling or give coordinates?"

Level 3: dependency repair. If SafetyWorker runs without weather, or WeatherFetchWorker runs without coordinates, it fails with a clear reason and the Planner routes to the missing prerequisite.

Shared State

AdvancedWeatherState in agents.py:

Field	Meaning
messages	Conversation history (user questions and final answers)
observations	Log of worker outcomes, success and failure (the Planner's main input)
attempts	Planner-level attempts per worker
steps_taken	Worker dispatches in this question (budgeted)
planned_for	Message count at question start, used to detect a new question
worker_instruction	The Planner's instruction for the next worker
target_location, coordinates	Resolved place and {lat, lon, name}
raw_weather_payload	Raw MCP weather tool outputs
safety_alerts, risk_report	Risk results
next_node_decision	Routing key read by dynamic_router in workflow.py
final_output	Reply returned to the user
Project Structure
weather_tool/
├── app.py          # Streamlit chat UI (shows the agent trace)
├── backend.py      # FastAPI server: POST /api/chat
├── workflow.py     # LangGraph graph + router + MemorySaver (unchanged)
├── agents.py       # Planner, 3 worker agents, tool-calling runtime
├── mcp_server.py   # MCP server: 4 tools (stdio)
├── llm_config.py   # LLM factories (Groq worker + structured planner)
├── .env            # API keys (not committed)
└── README.md
Setup and Run
Prerequisites
Python 3.10+
A Groq API key (or the provider your llm_config.py uses)
The worker model must support tool calling (openai/gpt-oss-20b on Groq does)
1. Install
powershell
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate
pip install langgraph langchain-core langchain-groq mcp httpx fastapi uvicorn pydantic python-dotenv streamlit
2. Configure .env
GROQ_API_KEY=your_key_here
NOMINATIM_USER_AGENT=WeatherPlatform/1.0 (your-email@example.com)
3. Run
powershell
python backend.py          # terminal 1: API on http://127.0.0.1:8000
streamlit run app.py       # terminal 2: UI on http://localhost:8501

The MCP server is not started manually. Each worker run spawns it over stdio.

Sanity checks
powershell
# list the tools exposed by the MCP server
python -c "import asyncio, agents; from mcp import ClientSession; from mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client
async def m():
    async with stdio_client(agents.mcp_params) as (r,w):
        async with ClientSession(r,w) as s:
            await s.initialize(); print([t.name for t in (await s.list_tools()).tools])
asyncio.run(m())"

Expected: ['geocode_location', 'get_forecast', 'get_daily_forecast', 'assess_weather_risk']

API Reference
POST /api/chat
json
{ "message": "Is it safe to fly a drone in Mumbai?", "session_id": "a1b2c3d4e5f6" }

200

json
{
  "logs": ["Planner","GeocodeWorker","Planner","WeatherFetchWorker","Planner","SafetyWorker","Planner"],
  "response": "Conditions in Mumbai ..."
}

logs is the ordered list of graph nodes that ran. With replanning you may see repeated workers (retries). Reuse session_id for follow-ups.

500 → { "detail": { "error_summary": "LangGraph Runtime Failure: ..." } }

Example Walkthroughs

"Is it safe to fly a drone in Dilli?"

Planner: needs coordinates → GeocodeWorker.
GeocodeWorker: geocode_location("Dilli") returns nothing → LLM retries geocode_location("Delhi") → success.
Planner: have coordinates → WeatherFetchWorker calls get_forecast.
Planner: need risk → SafetyWorker calls assess_weather_risk (for example wind 45 km/h → HIGH, NO-GO).
Planner: all data collected → writes the final answer.

"Weather in Xyzzyville" (cannot be found)

GeocodeWorker tries several queries and reports failed.
Planner replans: retry with a new instruction, or a final answer asking for the spelling or coordinates.
A second failure hits the attempt cap → an honest final answer. There is no fake fallback city.
Guardrails

Autonomy is bounded so it can't loop or hallucinate silently.

Guardrail	Value
Max worker dispatches per question	8 (MAX_PLANNER_STEPS)
Max planner-level attempts per worker	2 (MAX_WORKER_ATTEMPTS)
Max LLM↔tool rounds per worker run	6 (MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS)
Timeout per worker run	90 s (AGENT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
LangGraph recursion limit / backend event cap	40 / 30
Unknown worker name from the Planner	Treated as FinalResponse
Worker crash (MCP down, LLM error)	Becomes a FAILED observation, so the graph never crashes with a 500
Invalid coordinates from the LLM	Rejected by range validation
Final answer	Must be grounded in collected data and observations
Troubleshooting
Worker shows ExceptionGroup or AttributeError ... inputSchema. MCP SDK v1 and v2 use different field names (inputSchema vs input_schema, isError vs is_error). agents.py handles both. Make sure you are on the latest file.
Tool calling errors (tool_use_failed, 400). Confirm the worker model supports tool calling. Try another Groq model in get_groq_llm(model_name=...).
MCP stdio hangs. Never print() to stdout inside mcp_server.py. Use stderr.
Geocoding always empty. Check internet access and NOMINATIM_USER_AGENT. Nominatim blocks missing or generic user agents.
Planner keeps looping. The guardrails stop it at 8 steps. Check the terminal for the 🧭 Planner: <reasoning> -> <worker> lines.
UI says "Backend offline". Run python backend.py and check the Backend URL in the sidebar.
Limitations and Ideas
Each worker run spawns a fresh MCP subprocess (about 1–2 s). A long-lived MCP session managed by the FastAPI lifespan would cut latency.
The Planner makes one LLM call per step, so it is smarter but slower and costlier than a fixed pipeline. Use a small, fast planner model.
MemorySaver is in-memory. Swap in a SQLite or Postgres checkpointer to persist sessions.
Streaming graph events (SSE) to the UI would show each agent and tool call live.
Possible next tools: air quality, sunrise/sunset, hourly forecast, weather alerts, a no-fly-zone lookup.